{
  "abstract": "For a real primitive character $\\chi$ of conductor $q$, the Weil form $W$ of $L(s,\\chi)$ on odd test functions supported in $[-L,L]$ has a near radical: about $d=e^{2L}/q$ eigenvalues far below the rest, as Connes and Consani found for zeta. We show what planted-zero detection loses without it and give a rule for trial spaces. (1) [U] Every codimension-$m$ constraint transversal to an isolated $m$-dimensional near radical loses the near-radical term $K_{\\mathrm{NR}}$ of the detection functional $K=v^{T}W^{-1}v$, up to an explicit error. (2) Decision rule [num]: a trial space for detection or window-positivity certificates must contain the near radical. At ordinates 100–180, missing it loses a median 6–25% of $K$ when $d$ is 5–11, up to 44% at single ordinates; the near radical plus a band of 38–76 functions around the ordinate recovers a median 95–99% at the two runs tested. (3) [cert] Ball arithmetic certifies, unconditionally, 233 eigenvalues of $W$ on $L^2$ below $10^{-3}$ over 23 $(\\chi,L)$ pairs with $d\\le18.2$, the $k$-th at most twice its finite-space value. Exact: a Cholesky identity splits $K$ across frequency cuts, and under GRH, for large $d$, $\\#\\{k:\\mu_k\\le\\varepsilon\\}\\ge d-O\\big((Ld\\log(L/\\varepsilon))^{1/2}\\big)$ on $L^2$. Measured: the parity sectors alternate through the near radical, a Gamma-factor constant $(2a-1)/8$ accounts for the parity offset, the ladder density grows like $\\ln(\\pi d)$ (Landau–Widom), and the near-radical term is a median 89–98% of the part of $K$ that needs low modes. At two pairs, replacing the zeros below 110 by smooth points moves the depths by at most 0.3 decades. For the $q=5$ Davenport–Heilbronn function the near radical of the remaining-zero form carries the super-exponential growth of the first off-line zero's detection ratio.",
